M3.3: page-fault handler - first recovering exception + demand paging

- isr.s: exceptions gain a RETURNING path (trap_dispatch_common,
  mirrors irq_common): push 15 regs, call exc_dispatch, then - only
  when the handler fixed the fault - restore, drop vec+err, iretq and
  the faulting instruction retries. Park policy (dump_trap) stays as
  the fallback for anything unhandled or unmendable.
- trap.c: per-vector exception registry (32 slots). exc_dispatch:
  handler returns 1 = fixed (return to asm tail), else dump_trap.
- page.c: demand pager as the first registered handler (#PF):
  anonymous 4 KiB pages materialize on first touch inside a fixed
  128 MiB region above the 1 GiB identity map. One fault fixes all it
  can - empty PDPT slot gets a new PD page, empty PD slot gets a PT
  page, empty PT slot gets a zeroed frame from pmm_alloc - then
  flushes CR3. Present-bit faults and out-of-region addresses are
  declined (dump-and-park), and a 2 MiB leaf inside the demand region
  is treated as a conflict.
- main.c self-test: touch BASE (PD+PT+page), BASE+8192 (page only,
  same block), BASE+4MiB (PD exists, PT+page); values written before
  faulting are read back after the retry.

Evidence: "pf: ok faults=3 pd=1 pt=2 pages=3" - faults exactly as
predicted (3), both 2 MiB blocks sharing one PD (pd=1), a PT per
block (pt=2), three anonymous frames (pages=3), read-backs clean,
scheduler demo green after.
